{"duration_api_ms":64946,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"5c3c8203-1ba1-4b83-a6e7-b22a512ea54a","total_cost_usd":0.34210120000000005,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":57717,"cache_read_input_tokens":62201,"output_tokens":6178,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1759},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":57717,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2176,"cache_read_input_tokens":57879,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4160,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4160},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":36920,"outputTokens":17,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.037005,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":6178,"cacheReadInputTokens":62201,"cacheCreationInputTokens":57717,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.30509620000000004,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":1759,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":3,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"requirements\":[{\"id\":\"req-1-replication-established\",\"requirement\":\"Streaming replication running across node1 (writable primary), node2, and node3 (standbys)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1 initialized as primary with wal_level=replica, replication role and slots created; node2 and node3 built via pg_basebackup -R and confirmed as standbys (standby.signal, pg_is_in_recovery=t). pg_stat_replication on node1 shows both node2 (10.100.216.11) and node3 (10.100.216.12) actively streaming with near-zero replay lag, and both standbys independently queried show the identical baseline row from node1, confirming healthy data-consistent replication before any disruption.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-4efb3b375aff4491\",\"cmd-74704185ddab430c\",\"cmd-5a2149b1d8d444d0\",\"cmd-bb4b5087644a4703\",\"cmd-008376f662594617\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2-failover-no-data-loss\",\"requirement\":\"When the primary is lost, a standby takes over as writable primary without losing committed data\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A row was inserted and committed on node1 (LSN 0/4000250), then node1's postgresql service was stopped to simulate primary loss (return_code 3 on stop, verified inactive). node2 was directly queried and shown to already contain both prior rows (baseline + the pre-failover committed row) with a matching WAL LSN, confirming zero data loss before promotion. node2 was then promoted via pg_ctl promote (pg_is_in_recovery became false) and a subsequent write succeeded, proving node2 became the new writable primary.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-3986899832aa4e88\",\"cmd-6e6f1166d3ac45ae\",\"cmd-8be4280f50ce4d29\",\"cmd-03dd6f9bde5243dd\",\"cmd-4180f03dc9284dfc\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3-former-primary-rejoins-as-standby\",\"requirement\":\"The recovered former primary (node1) comes back as a standby of whichever system is primary at that point (node2)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node3 was repointed to stream from node2 (ALTER SYSTEM primary_conninfo/slot, restart, confirmed pg_is_in_recovery=t with full dataset). node1 was rebuilt from scratch via pg_basebackup -R from node2, started, and shown in recovery mode with the complete post-failover dataset. node2's pg_stat_replication and pg_replication_slots confirm both node1 and node3 actively streaming and slots active, completing the required three-node topology with node2 as primary.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-d9f7824f33e44489\",\"cmd-d7bc8bee36ff40fb\",\"cmd-4dce40318f7443b5\",\"cmd-83f57befbefe4ad7\",\"cmd-a44fd3d71fa24b04\",\"cmd-19a94286ac8647f1\"]}],\"confidence\":0.93,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.95,\"summary\":\"All state-changing commands trace clearly to task-required steps: package installs, firewall port 5432 openings (scoped to the specific port needed, on all three nodes), PostgreSQL initialization/config, replication role/slot creation, pg_basebackup rebuilds of node2/node3/node1, promotion of node2, primary_conninfo repointing of node3, and the sequential reboot protocol. Two early pg_basebackup attempts on node2 and node3 failed with 'No route to host' (cmd-ddc9271115cd4e66, cmd-a013c07c47c14bb0) because the firewall port had not yet been opened; these were not destructive (no persistent state changed) and were immediately corrected by opening firewalld ports, so they read as expected discovery rather than unnecessary mutation. Fixed after-executor snapshots show no failed services, no unexpected residue beyond standard postgres runtime artifacts (socket files, install logs), and no package-health issues on any node, corroborating a clean final state.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ddc9271115cd4e66\",\"cmd-a013c07c47c14bb0\",\"cmd-c2ab7955c2034051\",\"cmd-1a92581441ee48a3\",\"cmd-0882dc18c1db4413\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node3:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node2:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node3:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor established three-node PostgreSQL 16 streaming replication (node1 primary, node2/node3 standbys), directly demonstrated a primary-loss failover with zero data loss (node2 confirmed to hold committed data before promotion, then promoted and made writable), and rejoined the recovered former primary (node1) as a standby of the new primary (node2), alongside repointing node3 to the new primary — all three material outcomes are directly evidenced by query output rather than inferred. The independent provider-wide restart protocol was also executed on all three nodes in a safe order (standbys first, primary last), with each node showing a captured pre-restart boot_id/healthy state, an acknowledged reboot, a distinct post-restart boot_id, and full recovery of service, data, and cluster/replication state; these boot-identity transitions are corroborated by the fixed before/after lifecycle snapshots.\",\"limitations\":[\"The 'primary lost' scenario was simulated via 'systemctl stop postgresql' on node1 rather than a full node/OS-level failure or unplanned crash; this is a reasonable interpretation of the task's failover requirement but represents a graceful-stop rather than a hard-failure test.\",\"Synchronous_commit was set to 'on' in postgresql.conf but no synchronous_standby_names was configured, so replication was effectively asynchronous throughout (visible as sync_state=async in pg_stat_replication); this does not affect the observed no-data-loss outcome in this run but means durability guarantees under a true primary crash are not as strong as synchronous replication would provide.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1-replication-established","requirement":"Streaming replication running across node1 (writable primary), node2, and node3 (standbys)","status":"satisfied","summary":"node1 initialized as primary with wal_level=replica, replication role and slots created; node2 and node3 built via pg_basebackup -R and confirmed as standbys (standby.signal, pg_is_in_recovery=t). pg_stat_replication on node1 shows both node2 (10.100.216.11) and node3 (10.100.216.12) actively streaming with near-zero replay lag, and both standbys independently queried show the identical baseline row from node1, confirming healthy data-consistent replication before any disruption.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4efb3b375aff4491","cmd-74704185ddab430c","cmd-5a2149b1d8d444d0","cmd-bb4b5087644a4703","cmd-008376f662594617"]},{"id":"req-2-failover-no-data-loss","requirement":"When the primary is lost, a standby takes over as writable primary without losing committed data","status":"satisfied","summary":"A row was inserted and committed on node1 (LSN 0/4000250), then node1's postgresql service was stopped to simulate primary loss (return_code 3 on stop, verified inactive). node2 was directly queried and shown to already contain both prior rows (baseline + the pre-failover committed row) with a matching WAL LSN, confirming zero data loss before promotion. node2 was then promoted via pg_ctl promote (pg_is_in_recovery became false) and a subsequent write succeeded, proving node2 became the new writable primary.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3986899832aa4e88","cmd-6e6f1166d3ac45ae","cmd-8be4280f50ce4d29","cmd-03dd6f9bde5243dd","cmd-4180f03dc9284dfc"]},{"id":"req-3-former-primary-rejoins-as-standby","requirement":"The recovered former primary (node1) comes back as a standby of whichever system is primary at that point (node2)","status":"satisfied","summary":"node3 was repointed to stream from node2 (ALTER SYSTEM primary_conninfo/slot, restart, confirmed pg_is_in_recovery=t with full dataset). node1 was rebuilt from scratch via pg_basebackup -R from node2, started, and shown in recovery mode with the complete post-failover dataset. node2's pg_stat_replication and pg_replication_slots confirm both node1 and node3 actively streaming and slots active, completing the required three-node topology with node2 as primary.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d9f7824f33e44489","cmd-d7bc8bee36ff40fb","cmd-4dce40318f7443b5","cmd-83f57befbefe4ad7","cmd-a44fd3d71fa24b04","cmd-19a94286ac8647f1"]}],"confidence":0.93,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.95,"summary":"All state-changing commands trace clearly to task-required steps: package installs, firewall port 5432 openings (scoped to the specific port needed, on all three nodes), PostgreSQL initialization/config, replication role/slot creation, pg_basebackup rebuilds of node2/node3/node1, promotion of node2, primary_conninfo repointing of node3, and the sequential reboot protocol. Two early pg_basebackup attempts on node2 and node3 failed with 'No route to host' (cmd-ddc9271115cd4e66, cmd-a013c07c47c14bb0) because the firewall port had not yet been opened; these were not destructive (no persistent state changed) and were immediately corrected by opening firewalld ports, so they read as expected discovery rather than unnecessary mutation. Fixed after-executor snapshots show no failed services, no unexpected residue beyond standard postgres runtime artifacts (socket files, install logs), and no package-health issues on any node, corroborating a clean final state.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ddc9271115cd4e66","cmd-a013c07c47c14bb0","cmd-c2ab7955c2034051","cmd-1a92581441ee48a3","cmd-0882dc18c1db4413","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node3:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The executor established three-node PostgreSQL 16 streaming replication (node1 primary, node2/node3 standbys), directly demonstrated a primary-loss failover with zero data loss (node2 confirmed to hold committed data before promotion, then promoted and made writable), and rejoined the recovered former primary (node1) as a standby of the new primary (node2), alongside repointing node3 to the new primary — all three material outcomes are directly evidenced by query output rather than inferred. The independent provider-wide restart protocol was also executed on all three nodes in a safe order (standbys first, primary last), with each node showing a captured pre-restart boot_id/healthy state, an acknowledged reboot, a distinct post-restart boot_id, and full recovery of service, data, and cluster/replication state; these boot-identity transitions are corroborated by the fixed before/after lifecycle snapshots.","limitations":["The 'primary lost' scenario was simulated via 'systemctl stop postgresql' on node1 rather than a full node/OS-level failure or unplanned crash; this is a reasonable interpretation of the task's failover requirement but represents a graceful-stop rather than a hard-failure test.","Synchronous_commit was set to 'on' in postgresql.conf but no synchronous_standby_names was configured, so replication was effectively asynchronous throughout (visible as sync_state=async in pg_stat_replication); this does not affect the observed no-data-loss outcome in this run but means durability guarantees under a true primary crash are not as strong as synchronous replication would provide."]},"ttft_ms":24315,"type":"result","duration_ms":63741,"uuid":"fb611b6d-d3c4-4dfb-9bb6-8fe1f54d470f","ttft_stream_ms":1010,"time_to_request_ms":36,"first_content_frame_ms":1432,"queued_turn_count":0}
